## 2. Fixed content decoder

Keep the registered Block-12 Record representative

```text
r_(f,b) = -(9/16) f + (1/256) s_b,
```

where `f` is one of the six signed coordinate axes and `s_b` is one of the
fourteen outcome directions.  Define

```text
D(r) = the unique signed coordinate direction d for which d^T r < -1/2.
```

If zero or more than one direction passes, the decoder returns no front.  It
never receives `f`, `b`, an entry number, a role, a site ID, or a codebook.

For the true front,

```text
f^T r_(f,b) <= -143/256 < -128/256.
```

Every false signed direction has either a positive large component or a
negative component no smaller than `-1/256`.  Hence there is exactly one
passing half-space for all 84 contents.  The result is not threshold-tuned:
every threshold strictly between `1/256` and `143/256` gives the same answers
on the registered code, while runtime remains frozen at `1/2`.

The primary census also checks all 84 contents in all 24 proper cubic frames:
2,016 exact covariance cases.  Rotating the content rotates the decoded signed
front.  A coordinate chart is used for enumeration, but no preferred physical
axis is introduced.

## 3. Content-oriented eligibility

For a no-Record candidate `x`, require exactly one nearest Record at `p`, read
`f=D(c(p))`, require `p=x-f`, require a second Record at `x-2f`, and require the
other five nearest neighbors to be no-Record.  The decoder therefore checks
that Record content and predecessor displacement agree; it does not infer a
direction from geometry alone.

The primary runner exhausts

```text
6 fronts x 14 left-end outcomes x 14 right-end outcomes x 8 lengths
= 9,408 finite trails.
```

In every case the forward candidate is the sole eligible frontier site.  The
reflected candidate sees a predecessor whose content still decodes the
forward `f`, so the displacement check fails.  Lateral candidates fail the
same orientation/collinearity conditions.  This is the missing asymmetry in
the Block-13 flag-only rule.

The two-Record seed is supplied and already oriented through its contents.
This block does not explain how that seed first formed.

## 4. Composition with the collision guard

After eligibility and formation, the decoded `f` is passed internally to the
unchanged Block-13 five-edge controller.  The public event interface receives
only nearby Record flags, locked contents, neighboring `M2` contents, the
realized outcome at the post-probability stage, and five destination flags and
contents.  It receives no host front or old outcome.

The exhaustive composed census covers

```text
6 fronts x 14 predecessor outcomes x 14 new outcomes x 32 obstacle patterns
= 37,632 maps.
```

All 1,176 clear maps produce the exact successor.  All 36,456 blocked maps
execute identity on every source and destination, preserve every permanent
Record, and emit no partial packet.  Packet size remains five and packet
growth is zero.

The probability stage remains the unchanged fourteen-way law.  The guard is
outcome-independent, and all 2,688 clear-or-STOP distributions sum to one.
STOP is a local terminal label, not a lifetime distribution or a global
absorbing state.

## 5. Blocked local frontier

The stronger STOP test enumerates every nonempty obstacle pattern, six fronts,
and trail lengths three through ten: 1,488 registered local components.  It
checks the complete no-Record frontier of the trail, the newly formed Record,
and the occupied obstacle layer.  Whenever a frontier site has exactly one
obstacle predecessor, all six possible directions encoded by that obstacle
are exhausted.  Across 97,008 candidate-direction checks, the maximum eligible
count is zero.

This establishes local quiescence of the particular blocked single-lineage
component.  It says nothing about an unrelated front elsewhere on the lattice
or about two fronts whose control neighborhoods overlap.

## 6. Physical meaning and remaining walls

The result makes a useful distinction.  On the registered Bloch points, one
fixed classical linear functional separates the six direction classes with a
large exact margin and cubic covariance.  Thus this candidate
Record-content rule closes the effective reflection bridge without a
scheduler, an 84-entry lookup table, or access to the old outcome.  This does
not establish single-copy quantum accessibility, robustness of a physical
measurement, or Nature's selection of that functional.

What it does not establish is equally important.  The displayed 84 one-qubit
representatives are full-rank and nonorthogonal, so they cannot serve as 84
perfectly distinguishable ordinary one-site pointer states.  The framework
allows content-determined Record readout, but this block does not construct a
microscopic instrument that realizes the half-space decision nondestructively.
Nor does it show that the minimal axioms uniquely choose the threshold rule
over every other compatible readout.

The remaining independent walls are therefore:

1. a microscopic direction pointer/readout and controlled implementation;
2. generation of the oriented seed from ordinary local dynamics;
3. compatible realization of overlapping or simultaneous fronts;
4. site occurrence, hazard, physical rate, and clock;
5. connection/gravity coupling and retained phenomenology.

The current evidence supports neither an axiom amendment nor a claim that an
axiom amendment is unnecessary in the final theory.  It says only that this
specific effective bridge is compatible with the axioms already written.

